birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Wolckenhaar, Leonard","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Yáñez Fuenzalida, Nancy","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null},{"name":"Zimmerman, Eduardo","birthYear":null,"deathYear":null}],"subjects":[],"bookshelves":["Category: Law","Category: History and Archaeology"],"formats":[{"url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/88644/1/gplh-21-9783944773407.pdf","formatType":"pdf"},{"url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/88644/7/gplh-21-9783944773407.pdf.txt","formatType":"txt"}],"lastModified":"2025-03-20","summary":"Our modern legal system is based on the principle of equality. But is equality perhaps not also a concept that inadequately describes the complexity of normative orders? Highly differentiated societies with a multitude of collective identities and functional rationalities are in a permanent state of tension with this legal postulate. The contributions to this volume examine how this tension has developed in Europe and Latin America over the last 200 years","coverUrl":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/88644/2/gplh-21-9783944773407.pdf.jpg"}